# Expectation record for 02_constant_expressions/007_string_literal_constants.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_string_literal_constants
area               = 02_constant_expressions
description        = String-literal constants -- size as an integer constant expression, phase-2 splicing, phase-5 escape conversion, phase-6 concatenation, indexing and decay -- each folded property paired with a volatile-operand runtime twin
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
This program contains no undefined behaviour and depends on no unspecified behaviour, which is the
precondition both differential oracles rest on rather than a stylistic preference: undefined
behaviour would license either compiler to do anything at all, so a divergence between them would
prove nothing about either. The paragraphs below are the written argument required of every program
in this suite; the warning gate and the sanitizer gate are its machine half.
NO STRING LITERAL IS EVER WRITTEN THROUGH. Attempting to modify a string literal is undefined, so
every literal in this program is reached only through a const-qualified type. Where a mutable array
is needed -- bound_table, whose bound is sizeof of a literal -- it is a SEPARATE object, filled at
run time element by element from a literal, so the literal itself is only ever read.
EVERY SUBSCRIPT IS PROVABLY WITHIN BOUNDS, including the deliberate reads of a terminator index.
"abcdef" is an array of seven chars, so indices 0, 3, 5 and 6 are all valid and index 6 is the
terminating null byte rather than one past the end. escape_bytes has nine elements and is read at
indices 0 through 7, with its sweep loop running to sizeof escape_bytes - 1. bound_table has
sizeof "abcdefgh" == 9 elements and is written and read at indices 0 through 8, the last being the
terminator copied from the literal. Every runtime index is a volatile-sourced copy of exactly the
constant the folded line used, so the two paths address the same element. The two length helpers stop
at the first null byte, and every array they are given is null-terminated within its own bounds --
including the empty literal, which is an array of exactly one null byte -- so neither helper can read
past the end of an object. Pointer arithmetic is confined to a single array object in every case, and
no one-past-the-end pointer is ever dereferenced.
ONE ARRAY DELIBERATELY LACKS A TERMINATOR, AND ITS EXEMPTION IS EXPLICIT. C11 6.7.9p14 permits an
initializing literal exactly one element longer than the array, in which case the terminator is
dropped, so exact_fit -- declared char[4] and initialized from "text" -- is NOT a C string. It is
therefore never printed with %s and never walked for a terminator: a walk would run past the end of
the array, which is precisely the undefined behaviour this program exists not to commit. Its
run-time observation is an element-by-element dump across exactly its four elements, driven by its
own sizeof, and that is why it is the one object whose size carries no walked-length twin. The
exemption is stated at the declaration, with its reason, rather than left as a silent gap.
THE ADDRESSES OF TWO LITERALS ARE NEVER COMPARED. Whether identical string literals share storage is
UNSPECIFIED, so a program that compared two literal addresses would depend on unspecified behaviour
and its divergence would prove nothing. No literal address is printed, compared for equality with
another literal's, or subtracted from another literal's.
SEQUENCING IS EXPLICIT. Each volatile object is read exactly once, into a local, in a statement of
its own, so no call ever receives more than one side-effecting argument and no printed value depends
on the unspecified order in which arguments are evaluated: every argument is a literal subscript, a
dereference of const data, or a plain local snapshotted beforehand. No object is modified twice
between sequence points.
THERE IS NO SIGNED OVERFLOW, NO SHIFT, NO ALIASING VIOLATION AND NO READ OF UNINITIALIZED STORAGE.
Every arithmetic value is a small length, index or byte value and the largest printed number is 120.
Each fold_len line computes sizeof arr - 1u in the unsigned type sizeof yields, where the array size
is at least 1, so the subtraction cannot wrap and the narrowing cast to int is applied to a value at
most 11. The only division is an array's element count, whose divisor is a sizeof of a complete
object type and therefore non-zero by the standard's own guarantee, and no remainder operator
appears. Each array is accessed through its own element type -- the unsigned-char array only through
unsigned char -- so no object representation is punned; the mutable array is fully written before it
is read, every static object has an initializer, every local is assigned before its first use
including the agree accumulators, and nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of
unrelated objects.
EVERY _Static_assert IS SATISFIED and uses the two-argument form, because the message-less spelling
is C23 and the default gate's -pedantic -Werror rejects it. They pin the sizing and encoding facts
before the program runs -- that a literal carries its terminator, that adjacent literals concatenate
before sizing, that a character constant has type int, and that the escapes name the values the
lines then print -- so a mis-sized literal or a shifted character code is a translation failure
rather than a wrong number. That every one of them holds is a soundness requirement rather than a
preference: both mandated oracles compare the observable behaviour of a binary that built
successfully.
NO HEADER IS NAMED ANYWHERE and the single libc prototype the program needs, printf, is declared by
hand as `int printf(const char *, ...);`. bcc bundles the nine required freestanding headers plus a
bonus stdatomic.h and ships no standard I/O header (docs/project-guide.md line 212), so a directive
naming that header would fail on the bcc side of oracle (a) while succeeding on the reference side --
a spurious divergence caused by the test rather than by the compiler under test. Nothing in the file
is a GCC extension, so no include exception and no gate deviation is available to area 02 and none is
needed; the corpus has exactly two header exceptions, area 07's stdarg.h and the dedicated
bundled-header probe in area 12.
string.h is absent from that set entirely, so strlen is unavailable and every length here is
computed by a bounded walk written in the file -- which is an advantage rather than a restriction,
because the walk is the runtime twin that sizeof itself cannot have. Only ordinary narrow literals
appear: the wide and Unicode forms belong to area 11, which is where their support is investigated.
The source is pure US-ASCII.
THIS RECORD DECLARES NO WARNING-GATE DEVIATION: no ub_audit_flags key appears above, so the program
is audited under the suite's full default gate. It is clean under -Wall -Wextra -pedantic
-Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror in the single native compile-only invocation the
suite's warning gate makes, and it builds and runs clean under the sanitizer gate
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all run natively to completion. Both gates are
reference-compiler-only and are never applied to bcc, so requirement 3's shared-flag discipline is
untouched.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
This record narrows nothing. All four targets, all three optimization levels and all three
oracles are enabled, and no expected-divergence marker is attached. These notes exist to
record how that was achieved, because a program in this area could easily have had to narrow:
constant evaluation reaches straight into the type system, which is where the four supported
targets differ.
THE TWO-VARIANT RULE AND WHY BOTH HALVES ARE NEEDED. Every property is printed twice: once
from a constant expression, so the answer comes from the constant evaluator, and once from
volatile-qualified operands, which no implementation may read at translation time, so that
answer comes from the code generator. The mechanism rests on the language rather than on any
compiler's habits: an access to a volatile object is an observable side effect, so no implementation
may satisfy the runtime half by folding the value at translation time, and it must load the operand
and compute. Without that half, optimization substitutes the folder's answer for the backend's and a
code-generation defect escapes detection entirely while the suite still reports a pass. With it, a
divergent fold_ line accuses the constant evaluator, a divergent runtime_ line accuses the backend,
and a pair that disagrees with itself accuses one of the two.
The one construct with no possible run-time counterpart is sizeof itself, which every conforming
implementation evaluates at translation time for a non-variable type. Its twin is therefore the
WALKED LENGTH: every fold_size_ line is matched by a fold_len_ / runtime_len_ / len_agree_
triple over the same object, where the folded length is sizeof minus one and the runtime length
is a bounded walk to the terminator. Exactly one object is exempt, for the reason recorded in
the ub_notes above.
WIDTH NORMALIZATION. sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) were MEASURED at 4 bytes on i686 and 8
bytes on x86-64, AArch64 and RISC-V 64 (docs/technical-specifications.md target table, lines
457-462), so no plain long, no size_t, no ptrdiff_t and no pointer value is printed anywhere,
and no floating-point value is printed. Every printed size was measured identical on all four
supported targets -- the literal array sizes 1, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9, 11 and 12 and the sizeof-derived
bound 9 -- because sizeof(char) is 1 by definition, so none of them depends on a target's integer
widths at all.
sizeof yields a size_t, so every size in this program is cast to int before it is printed, and
sizeof is only ever applied to a literal or to an array -- never to one of the named pointers,
because sizeof a pointer would print a target-dependent width and manufacture a divergence out
of nothing. Where the source may place a string literal, and whether two identical literals share
storage, is left entirely free: the program never prints an address and never compares two
pointers for identity, so neither choice is observable.
PLAIN-char SIGNEDNESS. Plain char was MEASURED as signed on x86-64 and i686 and unsigned on
AArch64 and RISC-V 64. No plain-char value is printed and no printed line depends on that
choice, and that matters here more than anywhere else in the area, because a program about
character data is exactly where the difference would otherwise surface. Two mechanisms keep it
out of the output, applied together so the rule holds by construction rather than by inspection:
every character this program handles lies inside the 0x00-0x7F range, so its numeric value is
identical under either signedness choice, and every numeric print goes through an explicit
(int)(unsigned char) conversion in any case -- with the bytes carrying the escape sequences held
in an unsigned char array. Characters printed as characters go through %c, whose argument is
promoted to int and whose output is the byte either way, and the %c lines print only members of
the basic execution character set with positive values under both signings.
AREA SCOPING, AND THE SECOND SIGNEDNESS DIFFERENCE IT KEEPS OUT. Only ordinary narrow literals
appear here; the wide, UTF-8, 16-bit and 32-bit forms are investigated by
11_literals_and_strings/003_wide_and_unicode_literals.c. No wchar_t, char16_t or char32_t is
named in this program, which keeps a SECOND measured signedness difference out of its output:
wchar_t was MEASURED as signed on x86-64, i686 and RISC-V 64 and unsigned on AArch64, four bytes
wide on all four, so a wide element printed as a number would have needed exactly the
normalization the narrow bytes get here. No header naming those types is available in any case
-- wchar.h and uchar.h are outside bcc's bundled set and outside the corpus's two sanctioned
header exceptions -- so this scoping costs the suite no coverage; it places that coverage in the
record that owns it.
THE EXECUTION CHARACTER SET IS THIS PROGRAM'S PRINCIPAL IMPLEMENTATION-DEFINED PROPERTY, AND IT
IS PINNED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. Signedness and the character set are two different properties,
and the (int)(unsigned char) conversion above settles only the first: C11 5.2.1 leaves the
members of the execution character set and their codes implementation-defined, so the numeric
code of a letter is not fixed by the standard. This program observes a great deal of that set.
bound_table dumps the codes of a through h, giving 97 to 104. The terminator-free array dumps
all four of its elements t, e, x and t, giving 116, 101, 120 and 116 -- four values from three
distinct codes, because its initializer repeats a letter. fold_index_values and its twin print
97, 100 and 102 for a, d and f, and fold_char_digit_nine and its twin print 57 for the digit 9.
Six lines render whole literals as bytes -- text, onetwo, abcd, onetwothree, gamma and the
escape rendering -- four further observations render single characters through %c, and eight
escape codes are printed as numbers, each of those twelve appearing twice, once folded and once
recomputed at run time.
EVERY ONE OF THOSE CODES IS PINNED BY A _Static_assert IN THE SOURCE, grouped into three
assertions -- letters, digits, escapes -- so the accounting reads as three facts rather than
twenty-four. The letters pinned are a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h, m, n, o, r, t, w and x at 97, 98,
99, 100, 101, 102, 103, 104, 109, 110, 111, 114, 116, 119 and 120; the digits are 0, 7 and 9 at
48, 55 and 57; and the escapes are backslash, double quote, single quote, newline, carriage
return and question mark at 92, 34, 39, 10, 13 and 63, with the horizontal tab pinned at 9 by an
assertion that was already present. A, at 65, was already pinned too.
TWO OF THE NINE ESCAPE SPELLINGS NEED NO ASSERTION, and the distinction is worth stating because
it is a standard guarantee rather than a measurement. C11 6.4.4.4p7 fixes the value of an
octal-escape or hexadecimal-escape character constant NUMERICALLY -- the escape denotes the value
its digits spell, whatever the character set -- so the octal escape 101 and the hexadecimal
escape 41 are 65 by the standard rather than by the environment, and the two assertions relating
them to the letter A exist to prove the relation, not to establish their codes. The remaining
seven escapes are a different matter: 6.4.4.4p4 makes every SIMPLE escape sequence denote a
member of the execution character set whose value is implementation-defined, so all seven need
pinning and all seven have it. The null character is fixed at zero by 6.4.4.4p6 and needs no
assertion either.
ALL FOUR ENABLED TARGETS WERE MEASURED TO USE THE SAME ASCII-FAMILY BASIC EXECUTION CHARACTER
SET, so every pinned condition holds on every cell of this matrix. The assertions are what keep
the two oracles honest: on an implementation whose character set differed -- EBCDIC, say -- the
cell would FAIL TO TRANSLATE naming the reason -- accusing the environment, which is where the
difference actually lives -- instead of printing different numbers and different rendered bytes
that oracle (a) or oracle (b) would charge to the compiler under test. A future target that
disagreed would announce itself at the build, and the correct response would be to narrow this
record's target list with the reason recorded here, never to delete the assertions or the lines
they protect, and never to stop exercising string literals because their bytes are
implementation-defined. The same holds for a target on which sizeof(int) differed, which would
change char_constant_is_int's premise.
ONE ARRAY EXCLUDES EVERY CONTROL CHARACTER, AND THE REASON IS THE RECORD FORMAT ITSELF RATHER
THAN TASTE. Exactly one array in this program is rendered as BYTES on a line of its own -- the
escape rendering -- and it deliberately contains none. Excluding the newline and the carriage
return is obvious: either would move the output cursor and destroy the line structure a
byte-exact comparison depends on. The horizontal tab is excluded for a second and stricter
reason: the suite's expectation-record format FORBIDS a literal tab in any field, because every
recorded field is written verbatim into a report row, into a tab-separated summary column and
into a findings manifest, where a tab forges a column boundary and makes the record say
something other than what was measured. The line feed is the only control character the format
admits, and there it is the format's own joiner. A program whose stdout carried a tab therefore
could not have a conforming record at all -- and this was the one program in the corpus whose
stdout did, which is exactly why its record was the one that could not be written until the
array was corrected.
THE QUESTION-MARK ESCAPE STANDS IN THE TAB'S PLACE THERE, so the rendered array still holds six
escape-produced bytes and its size and length lines are unaffected -- sizeof reads 7 and the
walked length 6 -- and the substitution ADDS a spelling to the coverage rather than removing
one: \? is a simple escape sequence whose value 6.4.4.4p4 leaves implementation-defined, so it is
pinned at 63 alongside the other five in the escapes assertion, exactly as a rendered byte must
be. Nothing else is given up by the exclusion. The tab remains fully under test four times over:
its numeric code is printed from the escape array's first element folded, and again through a
volatile-derived index, printed again as fold_char_tab and runtime_char_tab, and pinned at
translation time by _Static_assert('\t' == 9). The newline and the carriage return are covered
the same way, through the fold_escape_newline and fold_escape_return lines and their runtime
twins. Only the RENDERING of a control byte is dropped, no escape sequence is dropped from
coverage by this arrangement, and rendering control bytes is not what this program is for. The
same convention is followed by 11_literals_and_strings/001_character_escapes.c, which owns escape
coverage in breadth and prints every escape it handles as a number and never as a byte. A sweep of
the goldens completed at this checkpoint -- 107 of the corpus's 108 programs, one golden each --
confirms this program was the only one ever affected, and that no golden the sweep covered carries
any control byte other than the line feed. The one record outside that sweep is named rather than
quietly counted into it: 13_floating_point/004_long_double_target_restricted.expected was still
being finalized when this note was written, so nothing is claimed here about its golden. The
property does not rest on the sweep in any case, which is why the count above is stated as evidence
rather than as the authority. require_report_safe_value in tests/conformance_harness/manifest.rs
refuses, at parse time and in every field of every record the parser reads, any character that
cannot appear literally in a report -- admitting the line feed alone, and only inside a heredoc,
where it is the format's own line joiner. A control byte in any golden, including one written after
this note, is therefore a hard parse error rather than a fact this paragraph has to keep up with.
A NOTE ON POINTER-ARITHMETIC SPELLING, since it looks like an omission and is not. C11 6.5.2.1p2
defines E1[E2] as identical to (*((E1)+(E2))), so subscripting a literal already exercises
pointer arithmetic on it. The explicit *(p + n) form is exercised as well, but through a named
pointer and through a decayed array rather than written directly on a literal, because both
reference compilers this suite supports must accept the program unchanged: one of them diagnoses
"literal" + n by default as a suspected concatenation mistake, and the default gate's -Werror
would turn that suggestion into a failure. Nothing is given up -- the same access is exercised
three ways instead of one -- and nothing is silenced: no pragma, no dropped warning flag and no
gate deviation, none of which area 02 sanctions in any case.
HERMETIC AND DETERMINISTIC. Every input is a literal in the source: nothing is read from the
clock, the environment, the command line, a file or a socket, and nothing is written anywhere.
Output is one line per semantic property claimed, in a fixed order, so a single divergent line
names the construct that produced it. No address or pointer value is printed, there is no
randomness, no locale-dependent formatting and no data-dependent iteration order, and the
program returns 0 -- well inside the 0-125 range the suite requires so that no exit status is
truncated by the operating system.
WHY ALL THREE ORACLES ARE ENABLED WITH NO NARROWING. Every line printed below is target-invariant
by the normalization argued above, so the same bytes are the right answer on each of the four
backends; that is what makes cross-backend comparison sound and lets oracle (c) hold these bytes as
the golden record.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
